<p>When a printer is equipped with a finisher that has the punch function, 
 holes can be punched in the printed documents.</p>
<p>Depending on the printer you are using, this function may not be available.</p>
<p class="Note">Note:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If the <span class="Term-Soft">Punch:</span> list 
 is grayed out, check that the optional finisher is installed on the printer 
 and selected on the <span class="Term-Soft">Accessories</span> tab of the 
 printer properties dialog box.</p></li>
<li><p>If the punch position you require is not available, 
 check the type of punch unit selected in the <span class="Term-Soft">Punch 
 Unit:</span> list on the <span class="Term-Soft">Accessories</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Available punch positions depend on the settings 
 selected in <span class="Term-Soft">Orientation</span> and <span class="Term-Soft">Print 
 On:</span> on the <span class="Term-Soft">Frequently Used Settings</span> 
 tab or the <span class="Term-Soft">Basic</span> menu of the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed 
 Settings</span> tab. Check these settings in advance.</p></li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box after 
 creating a document in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Frequently Used 
 Settings</span> tab or the <span class="Term-Soft">Finishing</span> menu 
 of the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Select the punching position in the <span class="Term-Soft">Punch:</span> 
 list.</p></li>
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
